# Layer Boundaries

## domain/

Pure TypeScript only. No React, Next.js, Prisma, or env vars.

```typescript
// ✅ domain/flows/types.ts
export const MONEY_FLOW_TYPES = ["OBLIGATIONS", "RESERVES", ...] as const;
export type MoneyFlowType = (typeof MONEY_FLOW_TYPES)[number];
export function buildMoneyFlows(...) { ... }

// ❌ domain — never do this
import { prisma } from "@/infrastructure/db/prisma";
```

Use `const` arrays + derived types, domain errors as classes, pure functions for business rules.

## application/

Use cases orchestrate workflows. External services via **ports** in `application/ports/`.

```typescript
// ✅ application/ports/open-finance.ts
export interface IOpenFinanceProvider { ... }

// ✅ application/use-cases/sync-orchestrator.ts
const provider = createOpenFinanceProvider(); // factory from infrastructure
```

Prefer extracting new orchestration into `application/use-cases/`. Existing pages may query Prisma directly for reads — when adding write flows or multi-step logic, use a use case.

## infrastructure/

Implements ports and wraps third-party SDKs. Only place for Prisma client, Better Auth config, Pluggy HTTP calls.

```typescript
// ✅ infrastructure/open-finance/pluggy/provider.ts
export function createOpenFinanceProvider(): IOpenFinanceProvider {
  return hasCredentials ? new PluggyOpenFinanceProvider() : new StubOpenFinanceProvider();
}
```

Stub providers must work without API keys so local dev stays frictionless.

## app/

Thin composition layer. Server Components by default; `"use client"` only for interactivity.

- Pages: `requireSession()` or inherit from `(app)/layout.tsx`
- API routes: validate body with Zod, `getServerSession()` for auth
- Authenticated pages: `export const dynamic = "force-dynamic"`
